theory.intro:
The **Trapezoidal rule** estimates $\int_{a}^{b}f(x)\,dx$ by replacing the curve with straight chords, so each strip becomes a trapezium. Adding the trapezium areas gives the estimate. It is used when $f(x)$ is hard to integrate, or is known only from a table.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!20] (0,0) -- (0,1.5) -- (1.5,2.1075) -- (3,2.58) -- (4.5,2.9175) -- (6,3.12) -- (6,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.6,0) -- (7.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.2) -- (0,4.1) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=0:6,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1.5+0.45*\x-0.03*\x*\x});
\draw (0,0) -- (0,1.5);
\draw (1.5,0) -- (1.5,2.1075);
\draw (3,0) -- (3,2.58);
\draw (4.5,0) -- (4.5,2.9175);
\draw (6,0) -- (6,3.12);
\draw[thick] (0,1.5) -- (1.5,2.1075) -- (3,2.58) -- (4.5,2.9175) -- (6,3.12);
\node[above left,font=\small] at (0,1.5) {$y_{0}$};
\node[above,font=\small] at (1.5,2.1075) {$y_{1}$};
