\node at (5.3,3.75) {$y=f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
For two strips,
$\begin{aligned}h&=\frac{2-0}{2}\\&=1\end{aligned}$
$\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}x&0&1&2\\\hline f(x)&1&\frac12&\frac13\end{array}$
$\begin{aligned}T_2&=\frac12\left[1+\frac13+2\left(\frac12\right)\right]\\&=\frac76\\&\approx1.167\end{aligned}$
For four strips,
$\begin{aligned}h&=\frac{2-0}{4}\\&=0.5\end{aligned}$
$\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c|c}x&0&0.5&1&1.5&2\\\hline f(x)&1&\frac23&\frac12&\frac25&\frac13\end{array}$
$\begin{aligned}T_4&=\frac{0.5}{2}\left[1+\frac13+2\left(\frac23+\frac12+\frac25\right)\right]\\&=\frac{67}{60}\\&\approx1.117\end{aligned}$
The four-strip estimate is closer to $\ln3$: its shorter chords stay nearer this curve.
$\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=-(1+x)^{-2}\\f''(x)&=2(1+x)^{-3}\end{aligned}$
On $0\le x\le2$, $f''(x)>0$. The curve is concave up, so the chords lie above it and both estimates are too large.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=2cm,y=3cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{ruleGreen}{HTML}{4F9B63}
\draw[->,black] (-0.12,0)--(2.25,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.08)--(0,1.18) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,domain=0:2,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1/(1+\x)});
\draw[ruleGreen] (0,1)--(0.5,{2/3})--(1,0.5)--(1.5,0.4)--(2,{1/3});
\draw[ruleGreen] (0.5,0)--(0.5,{2/3});
\draw[ruleGreen] (1,0)--(1,0.5);
\draw[ruleGreen] (1.5,0)--(1.5,0.4);
\draw[ruleGreen] (2,0)--(2,{1/3});
\foreach \x/\lab in {0/0,0.5/0.5,1/1,1.5/1.5,2/2}{\draw[black] (\x,0.02)--(\x,-0.02);\path (\x,-0.02) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\lab$};}
\path (0,1) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$1$};
\path (1.3,0.86) node {$y=\frac{1}{1+x}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The green chords show the four-strip approximation.

$\begin{aligned}h&=\frac{2-0}{4}\\&=0.5\end{aligned}$
$\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c|c}x&0&0.5&1&1.5&2\\\hline f(x)&2&\frac{\sqrt{15}}2&\sqrt3&\frac{\sqrt7}2&0\end{array}$
$\begin{aligned}T&=\frac{0.5}{2}\left[2+0+2\left(\frac{\sqrt{15}}2+\sqrt3+\frac{\sqrt7}2\right)\right]\\&\approx2.9957.\end{aligned}$
The curve satisfies $x^2+y^2=4$. The region is a quarter circle of radius two, so its exact area is $\frac14\pi(2)^2=\pi$.
Thus $\pi\approx3.0$ to one decimal place.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=2cm,y=2cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{ruleGreen}{HTML}{4F9B63}
\definecolor{ruleFill}{HTML}{DCEBE0}
\fill[ruleFill] (0,0)--(0,2)--(0.5,{sqrt(15)/2})--(1,{sqrt(3)})--(1.5,{sqrt(7)/2})--(2,0)--cycle;
\draw[->,black] (-0.12,0)--(2.25,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.12)--(0,2.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick] (2,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=90,radius=2];
\draw[ruleGreen] (0,2)--(0.5,{sqrt(15)/2})--(1,{sqrt(3)})--(1.5,{sqrt(7)/2})--(2,0);
\draw[ruleGreen] (0.5,0)--(0.5,{sqrt(15)/2});
\draw[ruleGreen] (1,0)--(1,{sqrt(3)});
\draw[ruleGreen] (1.5,0)--(1.5,{sqrt(7)/2});
\foreach \x/\lab in {0/0,0.5/0.5,1/1,1.5/1.5,2/2}{\draw[black] (\x,0.025)--(\x,-0.025);\path (\x,-0.025) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\lab$};}
\path (0,2) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$2$};
\path (1.55,2.12) node {$x^2+y^2=4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The shaded trapeziums lie below the concave-down quarter circle. Their total area, and hence the estimate of $\pi$, is too small.

